{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/hivemind/views/links.py | sed -n '42,90p;165,288p'\nrg -n 'def ask|transport|except' src/hivemind/jev.py\"","aggregated_output":"    42\t# anchor, links across periods only when the supplied text states how, and a base-effect example of the opposite sign\n    43\tREADS_V2 = {\n    44\t    CANDIDATE_READS: \"the CANDIDATE should be judged with the THESIS's current conclusion in view, because the THESIS's answer bounds \"\n    45\t                     \"or drives the CANDIDATE's: a part read against its whole (one product line's sales against the company's total \"\n    46\t                     \"sales; one region's loan book against the bank's total loans), or a quantity read against another it depends on \"\n    47\t                     \"(a component supplier's revenue against its largest customer's capital spending; an airline's fuel bill against \"\n    48\t                     \"its fuel volume) When either question could be judged with the other in view, the one whose answer the supplied \"\n    49\t                     \"text anchors less (no guidance, consensus or reported figure for the quantity and full period the question asks \"\n    50\t                     \"about) is the one judged with the other's conclusion in view.\",\n    51\t    THESIS_READS: READS[THESIS_READS],\n    52\t    NEITHER: \"neither answer bounds or drives the other: unrelated quantities (a retailer's gross margin and its store openings in \"\n    53\t             \"another country), or the same quantity for the same period and scope, or quantities of different periods when the \"\n    54\t             \"supplied text does not state how one period's answer moves the other's (a retailer's sales this year and its sales \"\n    55\t             \"growth next year, when the text only names both); sharing a company, industry or topic is not enough\",\n    56\t}\n    57\tSIGN_V2 = {\n    58\t    TOGETHER: SIGN[TOGETHER],\n    59\t    OPPOSITE: \"a higher answer to the question read makes a lower answer to the question reading it more likely (an airline's fuel \"\n    60\t              \"bill and its operating margin; a retailer's markdowns and its gross margin; one year's sales and the next year's sales \"\n    61\t              \"growth at a given next-year level, since a higher base lowers the growth)\",\n    62\t    NO_SIGN: SIGN[NO_SIGN],\n    63\t}\n    64\tQUESTION_SETS = {'links-v1': (READS, SIGN), 'links-v2': (READS_V2, SIGN_V2)}     # config.LINK_QUESTIONS names the same versions\n    65\tREADS_INSTRUCTIONS = (\"Should one of these two questions be judged with the other's current conclusion in view? Use only the supplied \"\n    66\t                      \"text. THESIS:\\n\")\n    67\tSIGN_INSTRUCTIONS = {\n    68\t    CANDIDATE_READS: \"If the THESIS's answer turned out higher, which way would that move the CANDIDATE's most likely answer? Use only the supplied text. THESIS:\\n\",\n    69\t    THESIS_READS: \"If the CANDIDATE's answer turned out higher, which way would that move the THESIS's most likely answer? Use only the supplied text. THESIS:\\n\",\n    70\t}\n    71\t\n    72\t\n    73\tclass Refused(RuntimeError):\n    74\t    \"\"\"The link step cannot finish for this article (a persistent circle, an unbound waiting row, a Jev failure); `record` says why.\"\"\"\n    75\t\n    76\t    def __init__(self, message, record):\n    77\t        super().__init__(message)\n    78\t        self.record = record\n    79\t\n    80\t\n    81\tdef reads_question(text, excluded=None, note='', version='links-v1'):\n    82\t    criteria = {k: v for k, v in QUESTION_SETS[version][0].items() if k != excluded}\n    83\t    return {'type': 'choice', 'criteria': criteria, 'instructions': READS_INSTRUCTIONS.replace('THESIS:\\n', (note + ' ' if note else '') + 'THESIS:\\n') + text}\n    84\t\n    85\t\n    86\tdef sign_question(text, direction, version='links-v1'):\n    87\t    return {'type': 'choice', 'criteria': dict(QUESTION_SETS[version][1]), 'instructions': SIGN_INSTRUCTIONS[direction] + text}\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\t# ---------- identity: which ladders are number ladders, and the label ----------\n   165\tdef stored_edges(conn):\n   166\t    \"\"\"Every stored dependency at its effective target (a bound wait counts through the question it is bound to).\"\"\"\n   167\t    edges = {}\n   168\t    for (view_id,) in conn.execute('select distinct view_id from dependencies'):\n   169\t        for dep in store.dependencies_of(conn, view_id):\n   170\t            if dep['resolved_to']:\n   171\t                edges.setdefault(view_id, set()).add(dep['resolved_to'])\n   172\t    return edges\n   173\t\n   174\t\n   175\tdef unbound_waits(conn):\n   176\t    return conn.execute(\"select count(*) from dependencies where coalesce(depends_on, '')='' and coalesce(wait_year, '')!='' \"\n   177\t                        \"and dependency_id not in (select dependency_id from dependency_resolutions)\").fetchone()[0]\n   178\t\n   179\t\n   180\tdef circles(conn, proposed):\n   181\t    \"\"\"The proposed links (reader, read) that lie on a circle of the stored relation plus every proposed link, each with one path.\"\"\"\n   182\t    graph = {k: set(v) for k, v in stored_edges(conn).items()}\n   183\t    for link in proposed:\n   184\t        graph.setdefault(link['reader'], set()).add(link['read'])\n   185\t    found = []\n   186\t    for link in proposed:\n   187\t        stack, seen = [(link['read'], [link['reader'], link['read']])], set()\n   188\t        while stack:\n   189\t            node, path = stack.pop()\n   190\t            if node == link['reader']:\n   191\t                found.append((link, path))\n   192\t                break\n   193\t            if node in seen:\n   194\t                continue\n   195\t            seen.add(node)\n   196\t            stack.extend((nxt, path + [nxt]) for nxt in sorted(graph.get(node, ())))\n   197\t    return found\n   198\t\n   199\t\n   200\t# ---------- selection ----------\n   201\t\n   202\tdef _ask(archive, cfg, jobs, transport, record, stage):\n   203\t    \"\"\"One Jev request per job, asked in order; each job is written into the record before it is sent (its request sha, whether the\n   204\t    archive held it) and completed as it is answered (its call id). A failure names the failing job's sha and leaves the later jobs\n   205\t    marked not asked.\"\"\"\n   206\t    entries = []\n   207\t    for state, questions in jobs:\n   208\t        _, sha = model.request_identity(jev.request_for(cfg.matching, state, questions))\n   209\t        entries.append({'stage': stage, 'request_sha256': sha, 'questions': sorted(questions), 'archived_before': bool(archive.lookup(sha)),\n   210\t                        'status': 'not asked'})\n   211\t    record['calls'].extend(entries)\n   212\t    replies = []\n   213\t    for entry, (state, questions) in zip(entries, jobs):\n   214\t        try:\n   215\t            reply = jev.ask(archive, cfg, cfg.matching, state, questions, 'match', transport=transport)\n   216\t        except Exception as error:\n   217\t            entry['status'] = 'failed'\n   218\t            record['error'] = f'{type(error).__name__}: {error}'[:300]\n   219\t            record['failed_request_sha256'] = entry['request_sha256']\n   220\t            raise Refused(f'link question failed: {record[\"error\"]}', record) from error\n   221\t        entry['status'], entry['call_id'] = 'answered', reply.call_id\n   222\t        replies.append(reply.data)\n   223\t    return replies\n   224\t\n   225\t\n   226\tdef _passes(answer, accepted):\n   227\t    choice = answer['choice']\n   228\t    return choice if choice in accepted and answer['probabilities'][choice] >= THRESHOLD else None\n   229\t\n   230\t\n   231\tdef choose(archive, cfg, conn, handover, checked, lists, shown_views, *, transport=None):\n   232\t    \"\"\"The links this article proposes: [{'reader', 'read', 'candidate', 'direction', 'sign'}], reader and read being a candidate id\n   233\t    (canonical) or a stored view id; and the record of how they were chosen. Raises Refused for an unbound waiting row, a Jev\n   234\t    failure or a circle that remains after one correction (PRD 06).\"\"\"\n   235\t    record = {'pairs': [], 'calls': [], 'corrections': [], 'proposed': []}\n   236\t    if unbound_waits(conn):\n   237\t        record['error'] = 'the store holds an unbound waiting dependency row'\n   238\t        raise Refused(record['error'], record)\n   239\t    aliases, problems = identity.batch_plan(handover.candidates, checked.outcomes)\n   240\t    if problems:                                   # the writer refuses the same plan (WriteConflict); nothing to ask\n   241\t        record['skipped'] = problems\n   242\t        return [], record\n   243\t    evidence = handover.evidence\n   244\t    stage1, asked = [], []\n   245\t    for c in handover.candidates:\n   246\t        outcome, target, _ = checked.outcomes.get(c['id'], ('skip', None, []))\n   247\t        if outcome not in ('new', 'succession') or aliases.get(c['id']) != c['id']:\n   248\t            continue\n   249\t        rungs = candidate_rungs(conn, c, outcome, target)\n   250\t        if not is_number_question(c['kind'], rungs, c.get('measure')):\n   251\t            record['pairs'].append({'candidate': c['id'], 'eligible': False, 'why': 'not a number question'})\n   252\t            continue\n   253\t        listed = list(dict.fromkeys(list(lists.candidate_lists.get(c['id'], [])) + [v['view_id'] for v in shown_views]))\n   254\t        theses = []\n   255\t        for view_id in listed:\n   256\t            ok = store.is_open(conn, view_id) and stored_is_number_question(conn, view_id)\n   257\t            record['pairs'].append({'candidate': c['id'], 'thesis': view_id, 'eligible': ok})\n   258\t            if ok:\n   259\t                theses.append(view_id)\n   260\t        if theses:\n   261\t            state = jev_matching.candidate_state(c, evidence)\n   262\t            stage1.append((state, {f'reads__{v}': reads_question(jev_matching.thesis_text(conn, v), version=cfg.matching.link_questions) for v in theses}))\n   263\t            asked.append((c, state, theses))\n   264\t    if not stage1:\n   265\t        return [], record\n   266\t    answers = _ask(archive, cfg, stage1, transport, record, 'reads')\n   267\t    directions = {}\n   268\t    for (c, state, theses), reply in zip(asked, answers):\n   269\t        for view_id in theses:\n   270\t            answer = reply[f'reads__{view_id}']\n   271\t            direction = _passes(answer, (CANDIDATE_READS, THESIS_READS))\n   272\t            record['pairs'].append({'candidate': c['id'], 'thesis': view_id, 'reads': answer['choice'], 'probabilities': answer['probabilities']})\n   273\t            if direction:\n   274\t                directions[(c['id'], view_id)] = direction\n   275\t    links = _signed(archive, cfg, conn, asked, directions, transport, record)\n   276\t    on_circle = circles(conn, links)\n   277\t    if on_circle:\n   278\t        links = _corrected(archive, cfg, conn, asked, directions, links, on_circle, transport, record)\n   279\t        remaining = circles(conn, links)\n   280\t        if remaining:\n   281\t            record['error'] = 'persistent cycle: ' + '; '.join(' -> '.join(path) for _, path in remaining)\n   282\t            raise Refused(record['error'], record)\n   283\t    record['proposed'] = links\n   284\t    return links, record\n   285\t\n   286\t\n   287\tdef _signed(archive, cfg, conn, asked, directions, transport, record, stage='sign'):\n   288\t    jobs, owners = [], []\n28:def transport(request, timeout=60):\n40:        except urllib.error.HTTPError as error:\n43:        except (urllib.error.URLError, OSError, http.client.HTTPException) as error:   # a dropped or truncated reply\n45:                raise model.TransportError(f'Jev transport failed: {type(error).__name__}') from None\n87:def ask(archive, cfg, setting, state, questions, purpose, *, transport=None):\n97:    response = (transport or globals()['transport'])(request)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-code-review/codex-r44.md\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-code-review/round44-brief.md\nls -l /opt/homebrew/lib/node_modules/@anthropic-ai/claude-code/bin/claude.exe'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Council round 44: are two code-review \"bugs\" real problems? (read-only, short)\n     2\t\n     3\tWork read-only. Read `AGENTS.md` (\"Authority and scope\", §3, §7.3) first. Report in English, briefly.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Context\n     6\t\n     7\tThe repository's owner, Alex, asked for a code review of commit range `95cf8da7..3ba40d8f`.\n     8\t- **What the commit adds:** a paused \"linking\" version (`src/hivemind/views/links.py`, poc-v119 and poc-v120) and a citation judge\n     9\t  version Alex chose not to adopt (judging-v12, poc-v121; D712–D719).\n    10\t- **What the product is:** `poc/config/poc-v118.json`, which uses neither.\n    11\t- **What the reviewer did:** reported two bugs and fixed them in the working tree, uncommitted. The diff is in\n    12\t  `docs/research/2026-11-22-code-review/review-fixes.diff`.\n    13\t- **Alex's challenge:** are these real problems, or nitpicking? Step back and converge.\n    14\t\n    15\tThe two \"bugs\":\n    16\t1. `scripts/replay.py` (and `clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py`) ran poc-v121 when someone named it. The fix adds\n    17\t   `refuse_unadopted_judging`.\n    18\t2. `src/hivemind/views/round.py` caught only `links.Refused` from `links.choose`. The fix records any other exception as an article\n    19\t   failure (`linking_failed`), as matching does.\n    20\t\n    21\tClaude's position, written before yours: `docs/research/2026-11-22-code-review/claude-r44.md`. Read the decisions it cites in\n    22\t`docs/DECISIONS-log.md`: D359, D694 (§0), D712, D719, D723.\n    23\t\n    24\t## Questions\n    25\t\n    26\t1. For each bug: is it a real problem for the product or for any named use? Name the concrete failure, who would hit it, and how\n    27\t   likely it is. Is the fix right, harmful or unnecessary? Check Claude's claim that the second fix can turn a budget-ceiling stop\n    28\t   inside the link step into an article failure that lets a run continue. Read `round.py`, `links.py` and the budget transport in\n    29\t   `budget_replay.py`.\n    30\t2. Is any of the eight \"recorded, not fixed\" findings in D723 a real product risk? Answer in one line each, or \"none\".\n    31\t3. Step back: does `3ba40d8f` change poc-v118's behaviour in any way? Look at the shared files: `judging.py`, `model.py`\n    32\t   (`check_schema` `maxItems`), `scripts/preflight.py`, `store.py`, `writing.py`, `round.py`, `config.py`, `parallel_day.py`. Cite\n    33\t   what you read.\n    34\t4. The converged action: keep, revert or change each of the two fixes and the three clean-ups. Add nothing new unless it is a real\n    35\t   product risk.\n    36\t\n    37\tOut of scope: redesigning the linking version, the frozen ruler, prompts.\n    38\t\n    39\t## Output\n    40\t\n    41\t- A verdict line per bug: REAL, NOT A PROBLEM, or FIX HARMFUL.\n    42\t- Answers to 1–4, each citing a file and line.\n    43\t- One converged action list.\n-rwxr-xr-x@ 2 lchingen  admin  323686048 Nov  9 11:48 /opt/homebrew/lib/node_modules/@anthropic-ai/claude-code/bin/claude.exe\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
